WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.254
Most visual question answering models are black
boxes: they output an answer,

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.088
but not the reasoning that produced it.

00:00:08.168 --> 00:00:11.922
This paper,

00:00:12.002 --> 00:00:15.757
Towards Reasoning-Aware Explainable VQA from the
University of Illinois and Amazon Alexa AI,

00:00:15.837 --> 00:00:19.591
augments a state-of-the-art coarse-to-fine VQA
backbone with an end-to-end explanation generation module,

00:00:19.671 --> 00:00:23.425
so the model produces a human-readable textual
explanation alongside every answer while

00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:27.259
keeping accuracy essentially unchanged.

00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:31.093
It compares LSTM and Transformer decoders as the
explanation generator, and,

00:00:31.173 --> 00:00:34.928
crucially,

00:00:35.008 --> 00:00:38.762
shows through a large human study that the standard
string-matching metrics like BLEU

00:00:38.842 --> 00:00:42.596
and ROUGE are unreliable for judging explanations,

00:00:42.676 --> 00:00:46.430
motivating better evaluation.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.608
In the classic visual question answering task,

00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:53.361
a model takes an image and a question and returns
an answer.

00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:57.052
The field has poured enormous effort into raising
accuracy,

00:00:57.132 --> 00:01:00.243
thanks to large pre-trained vision-language models,

00:01:00.323 --> 00:01:04.935
but almost no attention is paid to how a model
actually reaches its answer.

00:01:05.015 --> 00:01:08.375
The result is a black box: the prediction may be right,

00:01:08.455 --> 00:01:12.129
yet there is no evidence explaining the reasoning
behind it.

00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:17.008
The authors distinguish three kinds of models:
those that give only an answer,

00:01:17.088 --> 00:01:19.886
those that add a generic caption of the image,

00:01:19.966 --> 00:01:25.954
and the rare third kind that produces a logically
self-contained explanation matching the answer.

00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:30.583
Almost all state-of-the-art VQA models fall into
the first two categories,

00:01:30.663 --> 00:01:32.210
which motivates this work.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.753
Two open questions drive the paper.

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.996
First,

00:01:41.076 --> 00:01:45.239
can a VQA model generate a human-readable
explanation while still maintaining its answer accuracy?

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:49.482
Second, how good are those generated explanations,

00:01:49.562 --> 00:01:53.725
and how should we even evaluate them?

00:01:53.805 --> 00:01:57.968
Existing explainable-VQA datasets suggest using
conventional natural language metrics such as BLEU and ROUGE,

00:01:58.048 --> 00:02:02.211
but these were designed for string matching over
overlapping n-grams,

00:02:02.291 --> 00:02:06.454
not for judging whether an explanation truly
supports an answer.

00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:10.697
The authors argue that as reasoning problems in VQA
grow more complex,

00:02:10.777 --> 00:02:14.940
having an interpretable, well-evaluated explanation
is no longer optional but urgent.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.442
The contribution is two-fold.

00:02:19.522 --> 00:02:23.644
First, the authors present simple,

00:02:23.724 --> 00:02:27.847
easy-to-implement methods that sit on top of a
state-of-the-art VQA framework and maintain

00:02:27.927 --> 00:02:32.049
VQA accuracy while generating human-readable
textual explanations.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:36.251
Second,

00:02:36.331 --> 00:02:40.453
they provide both quantitative experimental results
and a large human study of the proposed explainable VQA method.

00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:44.656
Together these illustrate the urgency of proposing
new metrics to evaluate predicted explanations

00:02:44.736 --> 00:02:48.858
in vision-language reasoning problems like VQA,

00:02:48.938 --> 00:02:53.060
since the metrics in common use today do not
reliably reflect explanation quality.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.214
The architecture has two major parts:
coarse-to-fine visual-language reasoning for the answer,

00:02:59.294 --> 00:03:01.519
and an explanation generation module.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:07.000
A pre-trained Faster-RCNN extracts
region-of-interest features and image predicates such

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.930
as object and attribute labels,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.799
which are embedded with GloVe.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:14.972
The question is encoded with GloVe embeddings
passed through a GRU,

00:03:15.052 --> 00:03:19.082
and question predicates are extracted by filtering
out stop words.

00:03:19.162 --> 00:03:23.068
These signals flow through three modules:
information filtering,

00:03:23.148 --> 00:03:26.805
which removes noisy region information; multimodal
learning,

00:03:26.885 --> 00:03:33.158
which uses bilinear attention networks to fuse
image and question at both coarse and fine granularity;

00:03:33.238 --> 00:03:37.953
and semantic reasoning, which combines the two
levels into a joint embedding.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:45.240
That joint embedding is sent both to a multi-layer
perceptron for answer prediction and to the explanation generator.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.732
Two generator architectures are studied,

00:03:47.812 --> 00:03:51.157
a two-layer LSTM and an eight-head Transformer decoder,

00:03:51.237 --> 00:03:57.447
each trained with autoregressive cross-entropy loss
and teacher forcing on ground-truth explanations.

00:03:57.527 --> 00:04:02.990
The whole system is trained end-to-end with a
combined loss that balances the answer term

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.980
and the explanation term through a factor alpha.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.768
Because very few datasets provide annotated
explanations alongside answers,

00:04:10.848 --> 00:04:15.257
the authors chose the two largest available.

00:04:15.337 --> 00:04:19.745
GQA-REX contains explanations for roughly
ninety-eight percent of the samples in the GQA-balanced dataset,

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:24.233
about 1.04 million question-answer pairs spanning
eighty-two thousand images,

00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:28.722
with one explanation per pair.

00:04:28.802 --> 00:04:33.210
However,

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.698
its explanations follow the reasoning-format of
prior work and are not fully human-readable,

00:04:37.778 --> 00:04:42.187
sometimes containing grammatical inaccuracies.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:46.675
VQA-E provides explanations for about forty percent
of the question-answer pairs in VQA 2.0,

00:04:46.755 --> 00:04:51.163
and because those explanations are built by
matching captions to the question-answer pair,

00:04:51.243 --> 00:04:55.652
they tend to read more like image captions than
genuine reasoning.

00:04:55.732 --> 00:05:00.140
Both datasets have limitations, which the authors
are transparent about.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.864
The central result is that adding explanation
generation does not cost accuracy.

00:05:05.944 --> 00:05:11.424
On GQA-REX the model reaches a VQA score of
seventy-seven point four nine percent,

00:05:11.504 --> 00:05:14.814
and on VQA-E seventy-one point four eight percent,

00:05:14.894 --> 00:05:21.052
both essentially matching the baseline that was
trained without any explanation supervision.

00:05:21.132 --> 00:05:25.866
Varying the balance factor alpha changes the
trade-off only marginally.

00:05:25.946 --> 00:05:28.035
For the explanations themselves,

00:05:28.115 --> 00:05:32.442
the CFRF-plus-LSTM model outperforms the prior
baseline on VQA-E,

00:05:32.522 --> 00:05:38.206
with a BLEU-1 of zero point three three versus zero
point two six eight and a ROUGE-L

00:05:38.286 --> 00:05:42.342
of zero point three two five versus zero point two
four nine.

00:05:42.422 --> 00:05:47.291
The authors are candid that these absolute numbers
are only satisfactory,

00:05:47.371 --> 00:05:50.410
which sets up their argument about evaluation.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:55.380
The authors ablate two design choices: the balance
factor alpha that weights answer loss against explanation loss,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.050
and the choice of explanation generator.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.720
On GQA-REX,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.390
the LSTM variant ranges from about seventy-five to
seventy-seven and a half percent across different alpha values,

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:14.060
and the Transformer decoder at alpha equal to one
half reaches seventy-seven point zero six percent.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.730
On VQA-E,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.400
every configuration lands within a fraction of a
percent of the seventy-one and a half percent baseline.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:28.070
The takeaway from these sweeps is that answer
accuracy is remarkably stable no matter

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:32.740
how the explanation loss is weighted or which
decoder architecture generates the explanation,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:37.410
so the explanation module can be added essentially
for free.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:40.498
Four numbers summarize the paper's impact.

00:06:40.578 --> 00:06:46.805
The model achieves a seventy-seven point four nine
percent VQA score on GQA-REX and seventy-one

00:06:46.885 --> 00:06:49.062
point four eight percent on VQA-E,

00:06:49.142 --> 00:06:51.120
both at state-of-the-art level.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.381
In the human study,

00:06:52.461 --> 00:06:58.223
sixty-five point one six percent of the generated
explanations were judged by annotators

00:06:58.303 --> 00:07:01.011
to genuinely lead to the predicted answer.

00:07:01.091 --> 00:07:04.264
And in roughly sixty point five percent of cases,

00:07:04.344 --> 00:07:09.840
the model both predicts the correct answer and
generates a valid explanation for it.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.549
The human study itself was substantial: four
thousand seven hundred thirty-five unique

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:18.935
image-question pairs from the VQA-E validation set,

00:07:19.015 --> 00:07:20.993
each rated by three annotators,

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:25.640
for over fourteen thousand responses from one
hundred eleven subjects.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.984
The lasting message is twofold.

00:07:30.064 --> 00:07:34.028
Practically,

00:07:34.108 --> 00:07:38.072
explanation generation can be added to a
state-of-the-art VQA backbone with almost no loss in answer accuracy,

00:07:38.152 --> 00:07:42.116
giving users a human-readable reason alongside each
answer.

00:07:42.196 --> 00:07:46.160
But methodologically,

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.204
the paper shows through concrete examples that
string-matching metrics like BLEU and ROUGE

00:07:50.284 --> 00:07:54.248
can reward a wrong explanation and penalize a valid one,

00:07:54.328 --> 00:07:58.292
so they are unreliable for this task.

00:07:58.372 --> 00:08:02.336
The authors therefore argue that the real
bottleneck for explainable VQA is not the generator but the evaluation,

00:08:02.416 --> 00:08:06.380
and they urge the community to develop proper
reasoning-aware metrics for judging explanations.
